                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="814-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_o.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Omnidirectional" title="Omnidirectional" ><img id="814-1" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_c.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Cardioid" title="Cardioid" ><img id="814-2" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_8.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Bidirectional" title="Bidirectional" ></span>MXL 4000</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Multi-Pattern Condenser Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The MXL 4000 is a large-diaphragm multipattern FET condenser mic.</p><p>Part of MXL&#8217;s &#8220;900 Series&#8221; of mics, it sells for significantly less than its $799 MSRP. For example, street prices as of May 2009 were under $200. The &#8220;900 Series&#8221; is manufactured by MXL exclusively for Guitar Center and its various online sales divisions, including Musicians Friend, Music 123, and the Woodwind and Brasswind.</p><p>The mic has a single dual-sided, large-diaphragm, K67-style capsule measuring 25mm in diameter. It provides 3 pickup patterns, selectable via a switch on the mic body beneath the headbasket.</p><p>Additional switches on the rear of the mic body enable a pad (-10dB) and high-pass filter (-6dB/octave @ 150Hz).</p><p><img src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/mic_extras/mxl/MXL-60-shockmount.jpg" width="200" class="thumb shaft" alt="MXL-60 Shockmount" />The mic ships with an elastic shockmount (MXL p/n MXL-60) and a foam windscreen (p/n MXL WS-002).<p>Permalink: <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/MXL/4000">MXL 4000</a></p>
